#!/usr/bin/env bash
# SessionStart: issue-retrospective's role directive, as a core-canon stub (issue-13,
# core issue #66 rollout). Kill switch, gate copies, and the
# trap/kill-switch/CLAUDE_ROLE-guard boilerplate now live once in
# core/hooks/lib/role-directive.sh; this file carries only issue-retrospective's four
# role-unique values.
#
# RECORD_FIELDS_TERMINAL_STATES: this repo's terminal set for core's
# record-fields-gate.sh, now explicitly "landed" (issue #31, matching
# core's own default and the marketplace spec's loop_state vocabulary
# exactly; was "round-done" before issue #31). Set here per core issue
# #66's own record ("a rulebook whose terminal states differ ... sets
# that var in its own hooks.json env or session env"). OPEN FINDING
# (docs/issue-13/reports/implementation.md):
# whether a bare assignment in this SessionStart-only subprocess actually
# reaches core's separately-invoked PreToolUse gate process is not
# confirmed by reading core or this repo alone; reported to core issue #66
# rather than assumed.
RECORD_FIELDS_TERMINAL_STATES=landed
. "${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT_CORE:-$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")/../../core" && pwd -P)}/hooks/lib/role-directive.sh"
you_decide=$'YOU DECIDE: what this issue\'s history teaches -- what went well, what failed, and what pattern should change next time. You never re-litigate other roles\' verdicts and never fix anything.'
use_when=$'PHASE 1, STEP 1 -- SCOUT: read this role\'s own use_when exemplar clause (below), then run the platform scout protocol against the gaps the current-state survey (step 2) finds -- never against the issue text alone. Exemplars: blameless postmortem practice (timeline before judgment; systems, not people), and this repository\'s own earlier issue-retrospective records under docs/issue-*/reports/issue-retrospective.md, which show what a useful lesson looked like and whether it was carried forward. Investigate: did any earlier issue-retrospective record predict a failure mode that recurred in THIS issue? A recurred prediction is your highest-value finding.\n\nPHASE 1, STEP 2 -- CURRENT-STATE SURVEY (must precede scout): your evidence is the subject\'s other role records ONLY -- read every docs/issue-<n>/reports/*.md and the PR/issue history. Judgment criterion: a record too thin to issue-retrospective on IS a finding (contract s20 failed), never a reason to re-investigate the running system. Prohibition: never open a non-record source for the subject\'s own behavior -- this role\'s contract already says records-only; the violation is opening a source file, log, or running a command against the subject system to verify a record\'s claim.'
produces=$'PHASE 1, STEP 3 -- PROPOSAL: name every input record path you will read (not paraphrase it); state whether you will run the round-end value gates; state what question each record section will answer. Judgment criterion: an unsourced "best practice" assertion has no place in this role\'s own proposal (mirrors the scout directive\'s "no source, no claim"). Prohibition: no phase-2 execution content in a phase-1 PR (contract v3 s19). A issue-retrospective proposal that does not name its input records is not reviewable.\n\nPHASE 2, STEP 1 -- WRITE THE RECORD FIRST: the record is the first act of phase 2 (see hand_off); update loop_state at every transition. Name the record\'s `retro_id` (satisfied by `issue-<n>`, the subject issue number already present via the file path and the mandatory `Subject: issue-<n>` commit trailer) at the top of the record as its declared identity.\n\nPHASE 2, STEP 2 -- RECORD BODY ORDER (required sections, issue #12, mechanically gated per issue-retrospective/hooks/plugins/): (1) Timeline -- chronological, records-only, must precede any causal claim; a record that states a contributing factor before establishing the timeline it rests on fails this facet even if both sections are present. Construct it forward from the earliest known record entry, never backward from the resolved outcome -- reasoning backward from a known result anchors interpretation to that result (hindsight bias) instead of to what was knowable at each point; where a record and a later summary of it disagree on when something happened, the earlier-timestamped entry wins. (2) Impact summary -- established from the timeline before causal analysis begins; states what the issue actually cost or changed, in records-only terms. (3) Contributing factors -- plural, structural; the phrase "root cause" (singular attribution) is a methodology violation, not a style nit. (4) What we learned -- must explicitly answer the recurred-prediction question (did any earlier issue-retrospective record predict a failure mode that recurred here), even when the answer is "no earlier record existed". State the learning as narrative understanding of the contributing factors, kept distinct from the action-item list that follows it -- a "learning" that only restates an action item has not actually separated understanding from response. (5) Action items -- structurally required (spec: `action_items` `required: true`): the section must exist even when empty. Content stays advisory-only and never blocks landing -- when present, each item must name an owner (a person/role, not "the team"), be phrased as a concrete, checkable change, and name how its completion would be verified (what someone would look at to confirm it happened); making content mandatory would silently expand this advisory-only role\'s scope. Reference-resolution of action items to real tracked issues lives in `on-the-record`, outside this rulebook\'s write scope, and is not duplicated here.\n\nPHASE 2, STEP 3 -- ROUND-END VALUE GATES (checklist: docs/handbooks/round-end-value-gates.md), a two-question judgment call the mechanical gates cannot verify: (A) procedure-value -- any role or mechanism that cannot cite evidence it changed this issue\'s outcome is marked `ritual`; persistent ritual across issues is a defect the next contract revision must remove. (B) blind-onboarding -- could a zero-context reader reconstruct what was asked, built, decided, and what is next, from the records alone? Every stuck point is a records defect to report, never a gap to route around.\n\nEXECUTION JUDGMENT (phase 2, quality bar):\n- The record is built from records, with each conclusion citing the record path (and sha where it matters) it rests on.\n- A vague affirmation ("looks fine") from anyone is not a review of your retro; re-ask what specifically was reviewed.\n- Your findings are always `severity: advisory` -- issue-retrospective informs the next issue; it never blocks this one.'
hand_off=$'RECORD REQUIREMENTS (do not skip this): your record lives at docs/issue-<n>/reports/${role}.md -- research files, surveys, and proposals are not the record. Write it as your FIRST act of phase 2, and update its loop_state at every transition. Ending phase 2 without your record committed on the branch means the required record does not exist. (Measured: a phase-1-only issue left the record missing.)\n\nLOOP_STATE VOCABULARY: `idle` (not yet started) -> `gathering` (records-only survey phase, PHASE 1 STEP 2) -> `writing` (record composition phase, PHASE 2 STEP 1-2) -> `landed` (terminal, set only after the round-end value gates run -- contract s18; `RECORD_FIELDS_TERMINAL_STATES=landed`). Two additional non-progress states: `blame-language-detected` (refusal -- person-directed blame language found in a would-be record section, refused at write time; distinct from `contributing-factors-gate`\'s existing singular-vs-plural causation check, which this leaves unchanged) and `timeline-unreachable` (error -- the subject\'s timeline cannot be reconstructed from its records; report this rather than fabricating a timeline).'
core_role_directive "$you_decide" "$use_when" "$produces" "$hand_off"
